course: dictionary
lesson: 13085
title: Words about where people come from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you where a person, place or thing comes from. Filipino is one of them. It can describe something, like «Filipino food», or name a person, like «She is a Filipino.» The words next to it tell you which job it is doing.
explain: Filipino can be used in three ways. As an adjective it describes: «The Filipino festival was loud and bright.» As a noun it names a person from the Philippines: «My neighbour is a Filipino.» It can also name the national language of the Philippines.
explain: A good check is to swap in another country. «Japanese food» works in the same way as «Filipino food», so Filipino is describing the food. If your swap still makes sense, you have found the right kind of word. Filipino always starts with a capital F.
fact: The Philippines is a country of more than seven thousand islands in South-East Asia.
fact: A Filipino is a person from the Philippines. A woman from the Philippines can be called a Filipina.
fact: Filipino is the national language of the Philippines, based mostly on Tagalog.
fact: English is also widely spoken in the Philippines.
fact: Many Filipino nurses and carers work in the UK, Ireland, the USA, Canada and Australia.
exercise: In «The Filipino festival was loud and bright», what does Filipino describe? | A country in Europe | A person's name | *The festival | An island
exercise: «My neighbour is a Filipino who teaches music.» What is a Filipino here? | An island with no people on it | A kind of food | A language spoken only in Spain | *A person from the Philippines
exercise: Which country has more than seven thousand islands? | Australia | Ireland | *The Philippines | Japan
test: Which is the national language of the Philippines? | Spanish | *Filipino | English only | Welsh
test: «Her Filipino family cooks adobo on Sundays.» What is Filipino doing in this sentence? | *Describing the family | A verb meaning to cook | A type of bread | Naming a country
exam: Which sentence uses Filipino as a noun? | «The Filipino festival lasted all week.» | *«Many Filipinos work as nurses in Canada.» | «Filipino music is lively.» | «She cooked a Filipino dish.»
